Date: Sun, 08 Nov 2026 19:24:32 -0600
Subject: status site update!
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

[% FILTER html -%]
You may have noticed that something I mentioned at the
beginning of the year [1] just became real... Permalinks!
Archives!  This is feeling a lot like Wordpress wow. :)

Sorry to the RSS readers... I know HTML output is
still broken.  That is still something i'd like to
get fixed eventually :[  (... just not right NOW.)

Essentially each status update is a bunch of text
files, looks basically like what you see here.
(If you know Jekyll, Hugo, literally any sane SSG
out there, this is analogous to your posts directory
with Markdown with YAML frontmatter, except YAML
is just RFC-822 headers, and there is no Markdown.
(I have an ineffable distain for Markdown/YAML; don't
ask me why, but i'm just saying using said SSGs is
*NOT* an option for this site even if it'd make my
life a million times easier. :>))

Now you may be wondering "well how did HTML output
work before", I wrote the HTML output by hand (like
`m4 divert' with Template Toolkit conditionals).  I have
not worked on coming up with a more comfortable DSL for
semantic markup, hence the lack of HTML formatting as of
late.  (That is also out of scope for this "update"...)

Anyways, for the longest time, the whole site (with
its STAGGERING amount of... 4 files) was just built
from scratch, every time.  feed.html globs the data/
directory (where I keep said text files), sorts them
by date, and includes one's content.  (feed.xml does
the same except it only looks for specific headers.)
This is just a dumb source translation, meaning one
input file (the Template Toolkit file with the loop)
to one output file (feed.html and feed.xml).  To do
the same for 47 files (at the time of writing this)
would mean 47 sources (!) for each standalone perma-
link, which obviously had to be done differently...

(But okay, this bit isn't the hard part... you could
just, have an actual generic template with substitutable
variables.  I was just... lazy to write one :P)

The harder part is having build those 47 files every
time, which is... acceptable given the frequency at which
i post?  But you know I don't settle for less when I
can do better than that... :]  I am also VERY impatient
waiting for the whole site to re-build when I only changed
one single post 6_6 (unrelated to my name but... *ahem*.
I just absolutely can't stand being incorrect, to the
point my brain would shut down and refuse to collaborate
when things are in the wrong state.)

So I want dependency graph... but I also don't want
magic (black-box in other words, like most SSGs are).
But I also want the comfort of not having to worry
about things most of the time.  You want to know how
I did it?  Make! or any build system that works on files
really.  What I made was essentially a build script
generator that takes repetitive part out (like writing
the file names twice; the `a/%: b/%' that I have used
extensively for GNU make).  (I wrote it up during the
summer in my grandparents' house for my archive project
site (www-cellar), inspired by FreeBSD ports I'd been
seeing on my T480 (it's a whole-ass language written in
Make!  I'm not saying GNU Make isn't, but you just can't
deny that the `bsd.*.mk' headers aren't totally wack.))

